//? Title: Fermat Factorization: Exact Squares and Actual Trial Counts
//? Subtitle: Close factor pairs, finite budgets, full-width integer roots and owned same-pass history
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Given an integer, find a proper factor pair whose product recovers the input.
Divisors are easy to verify but can be expensive to discover. Fermat searches
for a difference of squares, making nearby odd factors especially accessible.
We begin with the three actual square tests that split 5959 into 59 times 101,
then retain the original two factorizations and seven factor-gap examples.
The native solver supplies exact states and counts directly. Prime inputs,
composite factors, a capped composite, large near-squares and optional capture
limits show precisely what each returned result establishes. Every figure is
produced by its complete editable C++ program.
)

\toc

> Three square tests and one rectangle

For odd n, test integer a from the exact ceiling square root upward. Put
d=a²−n and b=floor(sqrt(d)). The trial succeeds exactly when b²=d, giving
n=(a−b)(a+b). For n=5959, start at 78: 78²−5959=125 is not a square.
The next difference is 282, and the third is 441=21². Thus 80²−21²=5959
and the factors are 59 and 101. This takes three square tests and two
increments. Zero increments still means one test.

The native diagram uses the actual successful a and b for its equal-scale
geometry. The remaining L-shaped area can be rearranged into a rectangle
of sides a−b and a+b. Its area agrees with the factor certificate below.
The three boxes are actual captured solver rows, rather than a local search.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/fermat_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
