\alttext(A native equal-scale diagram shows an eighty-square with a twenty-one-square removed, a fifty-nine by one-hundred-one rectangle, and the actual three trial records for a equal to seventy-eight,seventy-nine,eighty. The final record is highlighted green.)

> Why the search finds the closest pair

An odd factorization n=pq, 1≤p≤q, gives integer
A=(p+q)/2 and B=(q−p)/2 with A²−B²=n. Conversely, a square hit gives
positive odd factors a−b and a+b. Every pair has A≥sqrt(n). Scanning
integer a in ascending order therefore finds the pair with minimum A,
equivalently the closest factor pair. That pair can contain composite
factors: for 945, the first split is 27 times 35. This is a splitting
algorithm, not a complete prime factorization.

Let A_min denote that first pair's A, and a0=ceil(sqrt(n)). Without a
trial cap, the exact number of increments is D=A_min−a0 and the number
of tests is T=D+1. The continuous excess A_min−sqrt(n) is not D; because
A_min is an integer, D=floor(A_min−sqrt(n)). For the returned pair,

\[
A-\sqrt n
=\frac{(\sqrt q-\sqrt p)^2}{2}
=\frac{(q-p)^2}{2(\sqrt q+\sqrt p)^2}
\approx\frac{(q-p)^2}{8p}\qquad(q-p\ll p).
\]

The approximation explains a small-gap quadratic trend at roughly fixed
p. It is neither an exact integer count nor a universal complexity formula
for any chosen factorization: a different pair may be closer.

> The original factorizations and seven gaps

These are the original two inputs and the original p=10007 with seven q
values. Small fixture primes are checked through native trial division;
this check is restricted to these small values. Native outcomes give the
actual factor and actual count. The integer formula uses the returned pair
and the library's exact ceiling root; it does not run a second search.

The first four gap cases take one test each. The old report called their
zero increments “steps”, obscuring that the square test still occurred.
The continuous guide below uses the stable rational expression above.
It is explicitly separate from the measured integer test counts.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/fermat_gaps.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
