> A trial limit says nothing about primality

Every odd n has the trivial pair 1 times n at a=(n+1)/2. If an exact
ascending search reaches it without an earlier hit, n has no nontrivial
pair and is prime. The TrialLimit stop says only that the allowed tests
ended first. Returning n alone cannot distinguish these two outcomes.
For 13, the first three tests miss and the fourth reaches the trivial pair.
For 5959, a budget of two misses and three finds 59 times 101.

The preserved legacy entry point uses a five-million-test cap. The composite
3000000021=3 times 1000000007 still returns n at that cap. There is no need
to run until its distant pair merely to know it is composite: the small
factor three is already an exact certificate.

The even branch returns two with zero square tests. For n=2 the other
factor is one, so proper_split is false. Integers congruent to two modulo
four cannot be a difference of integer squares; the even shortcut is a
separate operation. Odd squares succeed immediately with b=0.

