> Exact roots at the width boundary

A uint64 input can need a=2^32, whose square is 2^64. Keeping a²−n in
uint64 would lose that value. The core uses unsigned 128-bit differences
and the reusable integer_sqrt_u128 API. It brackets the root using bit
width and compares middle≤n/middle in an integer binary search. The
exclusive upper sentinel can be 2^64 in the wider type, so even the
maximum unsigned 128-bit value is handled without squaring that sentinel.

For odd uint64 n≥3, the trivial pair is reached by a=(n+1)/2≤2^63.
Every earlier tested a is no larger, b fits uint64, and the difference
fits unsigned 128 bits. The implementation checks the budget before
advancing an unused next a or trial count. These are fixed-width exact
integer statements, not floating error tolerances.

The former report's independent counter used ceil(sqrt(double(n))). At
n=(2^32−1)²+2=18446744065119617027, that expression rounds its starting
a down to 2^32−1. Its subtraction then wraps in unsigned 128 bits, and
its floating square root converts out of uint64 range. A strict sanitizer
probe reproduces that failure. This was a report-local counter defect;
the former core already corrected its initial integer root. The new report
has no local counter and reads every count from the solver.

The diagnostic floating ceiling below only displays that starting-value
mismatch; it never performs the unsafe wrapped subtraction or cast. The
exact wide API also handles arbitrary differences beyond Fermat's input
range, as shown by the final 2^128−1 root check.

