\caption(Quota,ownership and domain checks execute before comparing actual histories. Every completed odd trace has C=T; ordinary work obtains the same counts with no optional records.)
\alttext(A native count plot places actual odd-square,unequal-factor and prime histories on the diagonal C equals T,where C counts captured whole records and T counts actual square tests. Scalar metadata and allocation overhead are excluded.)

> Interpretation and sources

The native API preserves the original factor outputs and original full-program
stdout on their valid fixtures, while replacing the report-local counter with
actual solver work. The development tests freeze 4626 legacy factor lines and
compare independent divisor-pair counts, every captured square identity,
full-width root boundaries, budgets, quotas and ownership. An optional Python
math.isqrt oracle checks exact roots using arbitrary-precision integers; it
is a development check and is not a report runtime dependency.

The reusable additions live in number_theory_extra.hpp and
integer_arithmetic.hpp. Trial division and Pollard p-minus-one are unchanged.
Fermat's splitting method is distinct from Fermat's congruence-based probable
primality test. The next report covers that separate test.

C. Pomerance, [A Tale of Two Sieves](https://math.dartmouth.edu/~carlp/PDF/paper109.pdf),
Notices of the American Mathematical Society 43(12), 1996, pp.1473–1485,
particularly “Fermat and Kraitchik” on p.1474, gives the difference-of-squares
identity and its close-divisor advantage. The identity motivates this native
finite-budget variant; the count derivation, exact-width guards and capture
contract above describe this implementation.

Python 3.14's [official math.isqrt contract](https://docs.python.org/3.14/library/math.html#math.isqrt)
specifies the exact floor root used for the independent development oracle.
The native division-based implementation has the same mathematical root
contract over unsigned 128-bit inputs and uses no Python dependency.
